WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.244
A living crust is useful because it grows. That is also the

2
00:00:03.344 --> 00:00:06.475
risk. It must not survive outside the patch we put it on.

3
00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:08.572
So we build in a way to shut it down.

4
00:00:09.366 --> 00:00:13.586
The cell carries a toxin, MazF, that chops up its own messenger R-N-A,

5
00:00:13.686 --> 00:00:17.535
so it can no longer build protein and it dies. Alongside it sits

6
00:00:17.635 --> 00:00:21.361
an antidote, MazE, that binds the toxin and holds it harmless.

7
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.002
The trick is that the antidote is fragile and must be made

8
00:00:25.102 --> 00:00:28.431
constantly, while the toxin is stable. So the cell only stays alive

9
00:00:28.531 --> 00:00:30.888
as long as it is healthy and where it should be.

10
00:00:31.666 --> 00:00:34.735
There are two ways to trip it. A chemical signal can force

11
00:00:34.835 --> 00:00:38.340
extra toxin on demand. And because the antidote rides on a plasmid

12
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.727
that is slowly lost, a cell that escapes the patch dilutes its

13
00:00:41.827 --> 00:00:45.333
antidote away over about twenty generations and shuts itself down.

14
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.699
There is a bonus. The antidote only neutralises its own

15
00:00:48.799 --> 00:00:51.604
species' toxin, so if the DNA ever jumps into a

16
00:00:51.704 --> 00:00:54.756
wild microbe, that toxin simply kills the new host.

17
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:58.613
Now the honest arithmetic. One switch fails in about one

18
00:00:58.713 --> 00:01:01.851
cell in a hundred million per generation, which meets the

19
00:01:01.951 --> 00:01:05.202
standard target. But a single hectare of crust holds around

20
00:01:05.302 --> 00:01:08.212
ten to the fifteen cells. Multiply, and that is still

21
00:01:08.312 --> 00:01:11.733
ten million escapees per hectare. One switch is not contained.

22
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:16.485
The fix is redundancy. Two independent switches must both fail in the

23
00:01:16.585 --> 00:01:20.830
same cell, so their escape frequencies multiply, ten to the minus sixteen.

24
00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.648
Now the expected number of escapees on a hectare drops below one.

25
00:01:24.748 --> 00:01:27.584
That is what crosses the line at deployment scale.

26
00:01:28.366 --> 00:01:31.404
One caveat we do not paper over. The toxin only works

27
00:01:31.504 --> 00:01:34.542
in an active cell, so it cannot kill a dormant spore.

28
00:01:34.642 --> 00:01:37.857
The spore has to be woken first, and a stubborn fraction

29
00:01:37.957 --> 00:01:40.936
resists waking. Closing that gap is still open work.

